of MOFs were rarely reported. Herein, we embarked on exploring the possibilities of MOF shaping, from crystalline solids to transformable fluids, gels, and further into robust shaped bodies and foams. We presented a strategy for the continuous and reversible processing of MOF-based materials and successfully applied it into various MOFs. Specifically, by introducing HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) core-shell nanoparticles into carboxymethylcellulose (CMC) solution, we have achieved magnetic fluid (denoted as HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -MF) with high particle contents (25.0 to \(45.4~\mathrm{wt\%}\) ). Based on this magnetic fluid, a cup reactor was fabricated with solvent-induced hardening process. Moreover, a light and robust foam with hierarchical porosity was obtained by freeze-drying of HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -MF for efficient C-H oxidation (Scheme 1a). To study the feasibility of this strategy, we further synthesized a variety of foams from several important and distinct MOFs with high MOF loadings and low pressure resistance (Scheme 1b).

