The preparation of HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -MF is illustrated in Scheme 1a. Firstly, core-shell HKUST\(\mathrm{1@Fe_{3}O_{4}}\) nanoparticles \(\left(\sim\!100\ \mathrm{\nm}\right)\) were prepared through secondary-growth approach according to our previous report.7 Secondly, the obtained nanoparticles were pre-dispersed in acetone by ultrasound8 and then added into as-prepared transparent CMC aqueous solution followed by high-speed mechanical stirring. Flowable HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -MF, which could be drawn towards a magnet, was obtained (Fig. 1a). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern demonstrates that the underlying topology of HKUST-1 is retained (Fig. S1b). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and energy dispersive X-ray spec

troscopy (EDS) mapping reveal uniform dispersion of HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) in the fluid (Fig. S2c,d).

Remarkably, HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -MF is imparted with phase transition ability to reversibly transform from fluid to gel and to solid state. Upon immersing in small amount of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) \(\mathrm{(V(CH_{3}C N)/V(M F)\,=\,1/10)}\) for 10 min, the fluid was converted to a gel state (HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -gel), in which the material is soft yet loses its fluidity. It is worthy noted that thusobtained gel exhibits self-healing ability.9 Four pieces cut from a bulk of gel could be gathered together to reform the bulk material in just \(5\ \mathrm{min}\) by using a magnet (Fig. 1b). The gel material can be further converted to solid state by soaking it in five times its volume of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) . Using this method, the magnetic fluid is capable of shaping into desired form with a mold, such as bulk material and even stand-alone film (Fig. 1a,c and Fig. S4). Moreover, these shaped bodies are able to reconvert to gel and even to fluid state upon adding different amount of water.

