Specially, we fabricated a cup reactor through solidification of HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}\ @\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -MF (40 wt%, Fig. 1c) and then applied it for C-H activation catalysis without using any extra catalyst (Fig. 1d). SEM image and EDS mapping of the inner side of the cup indicate uniform dispersion of HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}\ @\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (Fig. 1d and Fig. S5). Diphenylmethane \(\left(0.250\,\mathrm{\mmol}\right)\) , tert-butyl hydroperoxide \(\left(0.625\,\mathrm{\mmol}\right)\) and chlorobenzene (2 mL) were premixed and added to the cup reactor. The cup reactor was subsequently heated at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in an oven for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) (a cover was put on top of the cup), and conv. \(76\%\) and sele. \(93\%\) was achieved (Fig. 1e,f). The product is ease of collection by pouring out without the need of separation of catalyst. It should be noted that the cup reactor could be directly used for the next catalytic cycle and the activity and stability of the cup reactor were still retained after three cycles (Fig. 1f and Fig. S6).

