To further explore the catalytic performances of other MOF-based shaped bodies, we also fabricated robust foams. We attempted to directly freeze dry HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}\ @\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -MF to generate hierarchical porosity. After freeze-drying of the fluids in a cylinder shaped mold, light foams with different densities

(o.058 to \(\mathrm{o.072\;g\;cm^{-3}}\) ) were obtained by varying the loadings of HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}\ @\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (Fig. S7a and Fig. S8). Taking the foam with 40 wt% for example, PXRD pattern indicates the retained phase of HKUST\(\mathbf{1}\ @\mathbf{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) (Fig. S9). Based on the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption isotherms at 77 K, the surface area \(\left(\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}\right)\) of this foam is \(196~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and the pore volume is \(0.65~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Fig. S7b and Table S1). Quenched solid-state density functional theory (QSDFT) calculation reveals the foam possesses hierarchically distributed porosity with a pore size distribution in both micro- and mesopore range (up to \(40\ \mathrm{nm}\) ). In comparison, CMC foam prepared under similar conditions shows \(\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}\) of \(24~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and a mesopore size distribution (up to \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) with a pore volume of \(0.06~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Table S1). The porosity of the foam was also verified by \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption \((60~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , 273 K and \(45~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , \(298\ \mathrm{K},\) Fig. Sio). As shown in the SEM image, this foam presents cell-like structure with macro-pores in micrometer range (Fig. S7c and Fig. S1). The above results demonstrate hierarchical porosities were successfully generated in the foam by freeze-drying approach. Besides, this foam with low density and magnetism can be readily attracted by a magnet (Fig. S7e). As proved by magnetism test at 298K, the magnetization saturation values of the foams (25.0 to \(45.4\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) ) at 10 T were measured to be 0.15 to 0.43 emu \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively (Fig. S7f).

Robustness is one of the critical prerequisites in practical applications, as it determines the durability of materials especially under harsh conditions. Proven by weight pressing, tape test and collision experiment, this foam possesses extraordinary mechanical stability and robustness (Fig. 2a,c,d). In contrast, the CMC foam shrunk to \(20\%\) of its original volume under pressing (Fig. S12). Furthermore, HKUST\(\mathbf{1}\ @\mathbf{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\) -foam can be cut into stand-alone thin film that is able to tolerate bending (Fig. 2b).

Given the excellent robustness and processing properties of the foam, we further set out to explore its applications in catalysis. This foam possesses extraordinary performance (conv. \(92\%\) and sele. \(97\%\) ) in the catalysis of C-H oxidation (Fig. 2f). It also shows higher reaction rate than simulated industrial pressing HKUST-1 pellet with similar MOF content as in the foam (conv. \(58\%\) and sele. \(98\%\) ) (Fig. 2e).10 After catalysis, HKUST- \(\cdot\mathrm{1}\ @\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -foam could be readily recycled for three times without losing its activity (Fig. 2f and Fig. S13).

